---
import GuideLayout from '../../layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import AdSlot from '../../components/AdSlot.astro';
const faqs = [
  { q: "Why does my PDF go back to sideways after I rotate it in my viewer?", a: "Most viewers only rotate the view, not the file. Rotate PDF saves the new orientation into the file so it opens the right way everywhere." },
  { q: "Can I rotate just one page?", a: "Yes. Each page has its own rotate button. Turn only the sideways pages and leave the others as they are." },
  { q: "Does rotating change quality?", a: "No. Rotation only changes a page setting, so text and images are untouched." },
];
---
<GuideLayout slug="rotate-pdf-and-save" faqs={faqs}>
<p><strong>Short answer:</strong> open <a href="/rotate-pdf">Rotate PDF</a>, select your file, tap the rotate button on each sideways page (or <strong>↻ All right</strong> for every page), then save. The new orientation is stored in the file, so it opens correctly on every phone and computer.</p>

  <h2>Why rotating in a PDF viewer doesn’t stick</h2>
  <p>
    The rotate button in Chrome, Edge, Preview or most phone viewers only turns the page on your screen. When you send the file, it arrives sideways
    again. To fix it for everyone, the rotation has to be saved into the PDF itself. That is what Rotate PDF does.
  </p>

  <h2>Step by step</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open <a href="/rotate-pdf">pdfsherpa.com/rotate-pdf</a>.</li>
    <li>Select or drop the PDF. Thumbnails of all pages appear.</li>
    <li>Tap a page to turn it 90° clockwise, or use the counter-clockwise button in its corner. Tap again for 180°.</li>
    <li>To turn every page, use <strong>↻ All right</strong>. Use <strong>Reset</strong> to start over.</li>
    <li>Click <strong>Apply rotation</strong> and download.</li>
  </ol>

  <AdSlot variant="inline" slot="guide-article" />

  <h2>Common situations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Scanned documents</strong> where a landscape page was fed in the wrong way.</li>
    <li><strong>Phone photos</strong> turned into a PDF that came out sideways.</li>
    <li><strong>Mixed documents</strong> with a few wide tables between portrait pages. Rotate only those pages.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rotation is lossless, so you can rotate as often as you like without blurring anything.</li>
    <li>Want to rotate and also reorder or delete pages? <a href="/organize-pdf">Organize PDF</a> does all three.</li>
    <li>If you are creating the PDF from photos, <a href="/jpg-to-pdf">JPG to PDF</a> straightens photo orientation automatically.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Privacy</h2>
  <p>The file is rotated in your browser and never uploaded.</p>
</GuideLayout>
